Built the next module, brother: the Living History Engine—with a tested Python reference kernel, an Iran pilot, cross-life extensions, and practical experiments.

HISTORY_STEREOSCOPE_PHASE_2.md⁠￼

The kernel tracks information encounters, compares claims at matching scopes, and preserves transmission ancestry. The wider module defines how to extend that into historical reconstruction.

What we can use it for—and learn from it:

Use	What it helps reveal	What we do with that
History and cultures	Different information, experiences and inherited narratives	Reconstruct why choices seemed plausible at the time
Parenting	The missing step between your understanding and your child’s	Explain that step, then observe what changes
Relationships	Different meanings attached to the same incident	Establish what each person noticed, assumed and needed
Wild Hawg	The gap between your product knowledge and a newcomer’s understanding	Test explanations with first-time viewers
Organizations	Where firsthand detail disappears between workers and leadership	Repair the reporting channel
Decision review	What someone could reasonably know before the outcome	Improve the decision process
Media literacy	Several accounts descending from one source	Count independent evidence accurately
Simulations	How different observation channels produce different behavior	Give agents situated knowledge and compare results
Ecology	How patterns persist through learning, inheritance or physical traces	Transfer useful structures while preserving their mechanisms

One useful lesson already appears in the Iran pilot: the history available to later readers can change because the archive changes. The State Department explains that its earlier Iran volume omitted CIA/covert-action documentation that the retrospective volume later addressed. That gives us a concrete way to distinguish newly available evidence from changed interpretation. 

The life extension has real anchors too: researchers documented humpback song types spreading between populations; NOAA maintains tree-ring measurements and chronologies used in environmental reconstruction. These offer different mechanisms of persistence and transmission to compare. 

The most powerful practical question becomes: “Which difference is driving this difference—and what observation would tell us?”

